         "detail": "Narrative prose is a clear pane of glass onto the story: the reader sees the events, not the writing. Clear and compelling, never ornate. NO purple prose.\n- Concrete and sensory over abstract and figurative. One clean image beats three stacked metaphors.\n- Plain verbs, few adjectives, shorter sentences, clear cause-and-effect.\n- Cut self-conscious rhythm, rhetorical flourishes, \"look-at-me\" constructions. If a sentence is admiring itself, cut it.\n- Window-pane is not flat — keep tension, momentum, immersion. Clear AND compelling.\n- Applies to all narrative beats; System panels stay terse and diegetic.",
         "requirement": "Prose style — window-pane (Alan, 2026-06-24)"
       },
-      {
-        "id": "entity-sync-on-publish",
-        "detail": "The GM boot reads each game-entity row as the authority for its sheet — the boot must never need file authority. commit-entity is the sheet-sync step: it upserts the entity row's canonical sheet. commit-state --controlled-entity merely points the live game-state at which entity the player controls (a relation), and writes no sheet. So a turn that changes a sheet (skill / affinity / equipment / level) MUST run commit-entity for that entity as one atomic publish alongside commit-state; a sheet edit committed only to the state relation, not the entity row, leaves the boot one turn stale.",
-        "requirement": "When a canonical entity sheet changed this turn, run commit-entity in the SAME publish step as commit-state — commit-entity writes the SHEET; commit-state's --controlled-entity only sets the game-state's entity RELATION. Different writes; never conflate them."
-      },
